1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,058
Tiny specks and squiggles drifting across your vision?

2
00:00:03,058 --> 00:00:04,410
You're not imagining them.

3
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:07,638
Doctors call them floaters.

4
00:00:07,638 --> 00:00:10,287
Their old Latin name: muscae volitantes.

5
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:14,373
Your eyeball is filled with a clear jelly called the vitreous.

6
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:20,520
Tiny clumps of fibers in that gel cast shadows on the retina.

7
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:21,658
That's what you see.

8
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:28,271
They drift when your eyes move — easiest to spot against a bright sky or white screen.

9
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:33,719
Very common, especially with age as the gel slowly gets more watery.

10
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:34,811
Usually harmless.

11
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:41,115
But watch for a sudden shower of new floaters, flashes of light, or a dark curtain.

12
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:46,463
They can mean a retinal tear or detachment.

13
00:00:46,463 --> 00:00:47,982
Get eye care the same day.
